!PC 3.09
========

!PC runs the PC card in a Risc PC.  This is everything it needs, for
installing from scratch or over an earlier version:

  !PC                 the application
  !PCConfig           its configuration editor
  Boot                additions for your !Boot (see below)
  PCDrivers           a DOS floppy image of the drivers for the PC side
  Docs.UserGuide      the PCPro 3 user guide: open PCP3MAN/html

!PC needs RISC OS 3.5 or later and the Toolbox modules, at least Toolbox
1.43, IconBar 1.18, Menu 0.32, ProgInfo 0.14, SaveAs 0.15 and Window
1.54; !PC says if they're older.  Networking for the PC needs the Internet
5 module too (see Networking, below).

3.09 is based on 3.08, by Dave Daniels, which was 3.06 changed to run on
RISC OS 6; the source of 3.06 was released under the GNU GPL by Aleph One.

Installing
----------

1) If you have an earlier !PC, back up !PC and !PCConfig, and your PC
   configurations, which are in Choices:PC.

2) Copy !PC and !PCConfig to where you keep your applications, replacing
   any earlier ones.

3) Copy Boot.Choices.Boot.PreDesk's two directories into your
   !Boot.Choices.Boot.PreDesk (Shift-double-click !Boot to open it):

     !GuardDD    stops you deleting a PC hard disc image (a DOS partition
                 over 2MB) by mistake.  Freeware by Aleph One; see its !Help.
     PCSleep     puts the PC card's processor to sleep at start-up, so it
                 isn't left running.  !PC also runs it after stopping RC5PC.

   Both are optional; restart the machine for them to take effect.

4) Run !PCConfig to make a configuration, then !PC.

The PC side
-----------

PCDrivers is a 1.44MB DOS floppy image of Aleph One's drivers, with its
own ReadMe.txt.  Open it in RISC OS (DOSFS) to copy them onto the PC's
disc image, or write it to a floppy:

  PCP3_Drv.CDROM    HPC_CD.SYS: CD-ROM for DOS and Windows 3.1 (with
                    MSCDEX or a DOS's own equivalent)
  PCP3_Drv.MOUSE    AMOUSE.COM: the mouse, for DOS and Windows 3.1
  Win3Vid           display driver for Windows 3.1
  Win95             display and CD-ROM drivers for Windows 95
  WinSound          sound for Windows 3.1 and 95
  utils             QUIT, MULTIT, SINGLET and SYNCTIME

The 3.0x boot disc these come from also had DR-DOS and its tools, SETUP.BAT
to install everything, and A. Walter's ASPI drivers; they aren't Aleph
One's, so they aren't here.  Use your own DOS, and copy the drivers to the
PC's disc by hand.

What's new in 3.09
------------------

- PCMem's dynamic area handler is C now, through a cmhg veneer.  3.08's
  wrote two flags to the wrong place in the RMA, which could put a page of
  video RAM in !PC's slot and crash it.  Seen on the first start of !PC
  after the machine started.

- The screen area is grown before the full-screen mode is set.

- "Middle button to PC", in !PCConfig's advanced options (MiddleToPC On in
  the configuration file): the middle mouse button goes to the PC, and
  Ctrl+middle gives !PC's menu.

- Mouse port 0x31B, command 9, latches where the ARM's pointer is, in the
  PC's pixels, for the PC to read at 0x318-0x31B.

- PCNAT, a network interface for the PC with no card behind it (below).

- !PCConfig doesn't overrun the resolution menu's buffer, and writes the
  'Last' file where !ChooseCfg reads it.

Networking
----------

PCNAT gives the PC a private network behind RISC OS, by DHCP: it is
10.0.2.15, the gateway is 10.0.2.2 and DNS is 10.0.2.3, which passes queries
on to the first of RISC OS's Inet$Resolvers.  Each connection the PC makes
becomes a socket of RISC OS's own, so RISC OS keeps its address and TCP/IP
(sharing a network card with the PC, as 3.08 did, can't do that).

It needs the Internet 5 module and the Mbuf manager, and RISC OS's own
TCP/IP set up and working.  !PC.PCBits.Netlinks loads PCNAT and then PCNE2,
which gives the PC an NE2000 (its IRQ is the configuration's NE2000Int).
In the PC configuration use

  NE2000Basic -pTCPIP

and load a packet driver or NE2000 driver for it on the PC.  PCNE2 uses
PCNAT's interface, pcnat0, unless Diva$SuggestedInterface or a -D option
names another: to put the PC on a network card of its own as before, set
Diva$SuggestedInterface to that card's interface.

*PCNATInfo says what PCNAT is doing.

PCNAT's SWI chunk (&5A4C0) is a development one, not yet allocated.
